veloper/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037937/assets/42df0f3278c4cf754f5e3c65787c2640a0c084ab1e79874dd3c071a97012b0dd.png)\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037937/assets/6c64f050494f28920cf5518293243a78d46e5fa2758fc8f470fdb466586effbc.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037937/assets/751725803c8443e8ccea32f24624965f57b739dab31251b837c5a86ecee96f43.png) terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037937/assets/fc13c27b687a2bfbf369fc03321ee89736bd1b2383871bf8ceac7457e2c35591.png)\n\nTherefore, the center is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037937/assets/dd3932b5cb055c4ae37e61384441deddc8626e4257ceb60b596325981b64710d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037937/example-6777.json" "file_sha256" "230bd948d5471ba55da2595fe97020e386bf6f2d9ec8106cce2d07caa892669a"} {"question" "e-5354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$4 y^2  - 10 x^2 - 8 y  - 40 x  = 76?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037937/example-5354.json" "file_sha256" "edc2d19d667201896481807b089a06edd2dda95a1e0bbb8fb625b6660aac5b41"} {"question" "e-5354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-10{x}^{2}-8y-40x & =76 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}-8y-10{x}^{2}-40x & =76 \\\\ 4[{y}^{2}-2y]-10[{x}^{2}+4x] & =76 \\\\ 4[(y-1)^{2}-1]-10[(x+2)^{2}-4] & =76 \\\\ 4(y-1)^{2}-4-10(x+2)^{2}+40 & =76 \\\\ 4(y-1)^{2}-10(x+2)^{2} & =76-40+4 \\\\ 4(y-1)^{2}-10(x+2)^{2} & =40 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{10}-\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center is $(-2,1)$." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037937/example-5354.json" "file_sha256" "edc2d19d667201896481807b089a06edd2dda95a1e0bbb8fb625b6660aac5b41"}] "usage" {"e-6775" [] "q-328355" [] "e-5354" [] "q-328358" [] "q-78379" [] "q-78860" [] "q-78254" [] "e-6777" [] "e-6776" [] "q-78868" [] "q-328296" [] "q-116082" []} "basis" 1289 "retractions" [[17592186083845 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $((x + 4)^{2})/(7) - ((y - 2)^{2})/(6) = 1$?"] [17592186082991 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{9} - \\dfrac{(y-2)^2}{4} = 1$?"] [17592186082991 :question/worked-solution "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h = 1$ and $k = 2$.\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(1,2)$."] [17592186082992 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{(y+3)^2}{16} - \\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{9} = 1$?"] [17592186082992 :question/worked-solution "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-(-3))^2}{16} - \\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{9}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h = 1$ and $k=-3$.\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(1,-3)$."] [17592186082993 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola\n$x^2 - 4 y^2- 2 x + 16 y = 31$?"] [17592186082993 :question/worked-solution "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4y^{2} - 2x + 16y &= 31 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x - 4y^{2} + 16y &= 31 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x - 4[y^{2} - 4y] &= 31 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} - 1 - 4[(y - 2)^{2} - 4] &= 31 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} - 1 - 4(y - 2)^{2} + 16 &= 31 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} - 4(y - 2)^{2} &= 31 + 1 - 16 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} - 4(y - 2)^{2} &= 16 \\\\\n((x - 1)^{2})/(16) - ((y - 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center is $(1,2)$."] [17592186082924 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola\n$4 y^2 - 10 x^2 - 8 y - 40 x = 76$?"] [17592186082924 :question/worked-solution "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4y^{2} - 10x^{2} - 8y - 40x &= 76 \\\\\n4y^{2} - 8y - 10x^{2} - 40x &= 76 \\\\\n4[y^{2} - 2y] - 10[x^{2} + 4x] &= 76 \\\\\n4[(y - 1)^{2} - 1] - 10[(x + 2)^{2} - 4] &= 76 \\\\\n4(y - 1)^{2} - 4 - 10(x + 2)^{2} + 40 &= 76 \\\\\n4(y - 1)^{2} - 10(x + 2)^{2} &= 76 - 40 + 4 \\\\\n4(y - 1)^{2} - 10(x + 2)^{2} &= 40 \\\\\n((y - 1)^{2})/(10) - ((x + 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center is $(-2,1)$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186082437 17592186082438 17592186082439 17592186082440 17592186082441 17592186263986 17592186263987 17592186263988 17592186263989 17592186263990 17592186264031 17592186264033 17592186264060 17592186264061 17592186264062 17592186264063 17592186264064 17592186264067 17592186264068 17592186264069 17592186264070 17592186264071 17592186283232 17592186283233 